<section class="hero">
  <img class="mark" src="icon.svg" alt="" width="88" height="88">
  <h1>Find what is tangled.</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    <strong>thicket</strong> analyzes a TypeScript codebase and emits a deterministic plaintext
    report of where it repeats itself and where its modules depend on each other in circles.
    You hand that report to a coding agent. It reports candidates — the agent does the judging
    and the editing.
  </p>
  <div class="cta">
    <a class="primary" href="#then-what">Run it, then what?</a>
    <a href="report-guide.html">How to read a report</a>
    <a href="example.html">See an example</a>
    <a href="https://github.com/alecf/thicket">GitHub</a>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="prose">
  <h2 id="quick-start">Quick start</h2>
  <pre><code class="language-bash">brew install alecf/tap/thicket

thicket &gt; thicket.md</code></pre>
  <p>
    No arguments: it analyzes the directory you are standing in, finding the tsconfigs itself —
    every workspace of a monorepo included, which <code>--filter</code> narrows and
    <code>--config</code> overrides.
  </p>
  <p>
    Or download a tarball from
    <a href="https://github.com/alecf/thicket/releases">Releases</a> and put the
    <code>thicket</code> binary on your <code>PATH</code> — a symlink is fine, it finds its own
    files through one. Prebuilt for macOS and Linux, on arm64 and x64; nothing to compile and no
    runtime to install, because the binary carries its own.
  </p>
  <p>
    Whatever it ends up analyzing, the report says plainly which files it did <em>not</em> reach
    and what argument would reach them.
  </p>
  <blockquote>
    <p>
    The download is a <em>directory</em>, not a lone file. thicket analyzes through the
    TypeScript compiler's native <code>tsgo</code> binary, which it spawns as a child process, so
    the tarball carries <code>tsgo/</code> beside the executable. Keep them together — thicket
    locates <code>tsgo/</code> relative to itself, resolving through symlinks, so installing
    anywhere and linking onto your <code>PATH</code> works; copying the binary out on its own
    does not.
    </p>
  </blockquote>

  <h2 id="then-what">You have a report. Now what?</h2>
  <p>
    Give it to an LLM. thicket finds the candidates and an agent decides what to do about them;
    the tool only does the first half, and a report nobody reads is a list of coordinates. There
    are two ways to arrange the second half, and the only real difference is who types the
    command.
  </p>

  <h3 id="hand-it-over">1. Run it yourself, hand the report over</h3>
  <pre><code class="language-bash">thicket &gt; thicket.md</code></pre>
  <p>Then, in Claude Code, Codex, Cursor, or whatever you use:</p>
  <blockquote>
    <p>
      Read <code>thicket.md</code>. It lists duplication and dependency-cycle
      <strong>candidates</strong> in this repo — it has not judged any of them, and some of them
      are not worth doing. The top of the file links to a guide explaining every field; fetch
      that first. Then pick the findings you think are genuinely worth acting on, tell me which
      ones you are rejecting and why, and do the top one.
    </p>
  </blockquote>
  <p>
    That link is what makes this work without you explaining anything. Every report opens with a
    pointer to the <a href="report-guide.md">field guide in raw Markdown</a> — one fetch and the
    agent knows what <code>L1</code> means, what <code>varies across copies</code> is for, and
    how to tell a finding worth acting on from one that merely scored well.
  </p>

  <h3 id="agent-runs-it">2. Let the agent run it</h3>
  <p>
    Nothing needs a human in the middle. It is one command with text output, so the agent can do
    the run too:
  </p>
  <blockquote>
    <p>
      Run <code>thicket &gt; thicket.md</code> and read the report — it
      links to a guide explaining how to read it. Pick the one finding you are most confident is
      worth consolidating, do it, then re-run thicket and show me that the finding is gone.
    </p>
  </blockquote>
  <p>
    If you do this more than once, put the command in the repository's own
    <code>CLAUDE.md</code> or <code>AGENTS.md</code>, so it is already in context whenever
    someone asks for a cleanup.
  </p>

  <h3 id="close-the-loop">3. Either way, close the loop</h3>
  <p>
    Findings are identified by content rather than position, so a second report says which ones
    actually went away rather than which code got touched:
  </p>
  <pre><code class="language-bash">thicket --json before.json &gt; thicket.md
# ...the agent refactors...
thicket --json after.json &gt; thicket.md
thicket diff before.json after.json</code></pre>
  <pre><code>1 finding resolved, 0 new, duplicated mass -65.2% (253 -&gt; 88), propagation cost 0.44 -&gt; 0.44
  - THK-DUP-c389b5be</code></pre>
  <p>
    An agent that reformatted the copies and declared victory shows up here as zero resolved.
  </p>

  <h2 id="what-it-reports">What it reports</h2>
</section>

<section class="cards">
  <div class="card">
    <h3>Duplication</h3>
    <p>
      Clusters of repeated code, ranked by the lines a successful extraction would actually
      delete — with the constants that parameterize each shape, and where the abstraction may
      already exist.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="card">
    <h3>Module tangles</h3>
    <p>
      Dependency cycles, drawn as a chart, with the one fact that decides how much they matter:
      whether any real file-level cycle underlies them, or whether they are an artifact of how
      files were grouped.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="card">
    <h3>Cheap fixes first</h3>
    <p>
      An edge whose imports are re-exported from elsewhere is routing, not dependency. thicket
      names those separately, because repointing a specifier is a find-and-replace and cutting a
      dependency is a decision.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="card">
    <h3>Byte-identical runs</h3>
    <p>
      The report is a pure function of source, config and version, so diffing report N against
      N+1 tells you whether a refactor actually landed. No timestamps, no absolute paths, no
      locale-dependent ordering.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="prose">
  <h2 id="not-a-linter">It is not a linter</h2>
  <p>
    Worth getting straight before you read a report, because everything in it is shaped by it:
    <strong>thicket is not scoring your codebase, and nothing it prints is a defect.</strong>
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>No grade and no threshold.</strong> No pass/fail, no “complexity: B−”, no exit code
      that means too messy. It exits 0 whether it found three findings or eighteen thousand,
      because “is this too much duplication” is a question about your deadlines and your team,
      and a tool that answered it would be guessing.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>The summary numbers are trends, not marks.</strong> <code>duplicated mass</code>
      deliberately double-counts nested clusters; comparing it between two codebases means
      nothing. Comparing it against itself one refactor later is the entire point of it.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>A finding is a candidate, phrased like one.</strong> <em>These 19 things are the
      same shape, here is every location, here is what differs between the copies, here is the
      base class all of them already import.</em> Whether consolidating that is an improvement is
      a design judgement — and the answer is frequently no.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    The split of labour is the whole design. A <strong>deterministic</strong> pass finds every
    exact and α-renamed repeat across the entire tree — identically on every run, with no context
    window and no sampling — and the model then spends its judgement on the few dozen candidates
    that come back, instead of spending it on the search.
  </p>
  <p>
    That ordering is also the cheap one. Asking a model to find duplication means feeding it the
    codebase, and cross-file duplication is the specific thing it is worst placed to find: the
    two copies live in two files it never held open at the same time. thicket reads all of them
    outside the model, costs no tokens doing it, and hands back a report with a token budget you
    set.
  </p>

  <h2 id="ai-written-code">Especially for code an LLM wrote</h2>
  <p>
    An agent writes what it can see. It cannot see the helper in the file it did not open, so it
    writes that helper again — correctly, sensibly in local terms, and for the fifth time. Each
    diff is fine on its own, a reviewer looking at one PR cannot see the other four copies
    either, and the next session starts with an empty context and does it again.
  </p>
  <p>
    That is precisely the shape this tool is built for: the same structure, in files that never
    appear in one diff together, often with every identifier renamed — which is what
    <code>L1</code> matching exists to catch. A codebase written largely by agents accumulates it
    quietly, and no amount of care inside a single session prevents it.
  </p>
  <p>
    Because the report is a pure function of the source, it is safe to run on a schedule — a
    weekly job, a cron, a manual dispatch — and compare against the last one:
  </p>
  <pre><code class="language-bash">thicket --json .thicket/this-week.json &gt; thicket.md
thicket diff .thicket/last-week.json .thicket/this-week.json</code></pre>
  <p>
    Then hand <code>thicket.md</code> to an agent with the brief above. Run it as cleanup, not as
    a gate: it reports candidates, so wiring it into a required check would turn it into the
    linter it deliberately is not.
  </p>
</section>
